WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.280
With a hilarious deleted post-credits&nbsp;
scene, a surprise ending for Camilo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.960
and an even darker fate for Abuela's husband,&nbsp;
the ending of Encanto was almost very different.
&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.360
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and&nbsp;
in this video I'm revealing the alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.680
endings for Encanto you never got to see!
Spoilers ahead of course, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.600
During the beautiful, melancholy song "Dos&nbsp;
Oruguitas", we get the full backstory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.040
Abuela and her husband Pedro, how they met, fell&nbsp;
in love, got married, had triplets and then how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.920
he tragically died trying to protect his family&nbsp;
and the townspeople from a group of soldiers.
&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.000
However, this scene was originally&nbsp;
going to be even more heart-breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.200
because in early drafts, Alma and Pedro had to&nbsp;
flee their town before the children were born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.360
and according to Encanto co-director&nbsp;
Jared Bush, "there was a version where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.720
she had the babies at the river and they&nbsp;
were born the same moment Pedro died."
&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.320
Now, Pedro's fate was already&nbsp;
pretty dark for a Disney movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.920
but I think that Alma giving birth as her husband&nbsp;
died beside her would have been even more bleak.
&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.320
There is a tiny trace of this idea though&nbsp;
in the final movie in the following line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.920
which Bush has confirmed does imply that Pedro&nbsp;
died on the very day the triplets were born.
&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.200
When my three babies had just been born, your&nbsp;
Abuelo Pedro and I were forced to flee our home.
&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.720
Thankfully they decided not to&nbsp;
go with this alternate ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.280
meaning Pedro did at least get to say goodbye&nbsp;
to his three children before his death. 
&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.360
Bush also revealed that Chispi the capybara&nbsp;
had a funny, fully-animated post-credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.920
scene that didn't make it into the movie.
In the deleted scene, Chispi lifts his leg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.360
to relieve himself on the casita, but the&nbsp;
magical house stops the capybara by tipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.080
a drain full of water on him, giving the&nbsp;
creature a taste of his own medicine. 
&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.320
Bush said the scene was cut because they&nbsp;
preferred to finish the movie "on the emotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.760
and joy of the family coming back together."
The mischievous casita can also be seen pranking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.800
a capybara in this unused concept art where&nbsp;
it tips the cute creature off a chair.
&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.720
In another alternate storyline, Mirabel had&nbsp;
a confrontation with her mother Julieta at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.960
the end of the movie rather than with her&nbsp;
grandmother as happened in the final film.
&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.240
In this version, it was Mirabel's anger with&nbsp;
her mother that led to the Casita's destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.360
as with each angry word she uttered, it was&nbsp;
as if a blow was being struck to the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.600
causing it to break apart.
The idea was that Mirabel and Casita&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.080
"shared a mental connection", so her rage&nbsp;
would end up wrecking the family home. 
&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.760
In another version of the story, Casita was&nbsp;
destroyed by fire, burning to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.920
rather than crumbling to pieces. 
It would have been especially poignant for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.960
a fire to destroy the magical house given&nbsp;
that it owed its existence to a candle.
&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.440
In the aftermath of the fire, the windows&nbsp;
and doors survived to give a sense of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.720
original layout of the house, and a spooky&nbsp;
ghost-like shadow was also going to appear.
&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.480
This was supposed to feel like there was a ghostly&nbsp;
spirit still in the ruins of the house, a shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.480
that haunted it, perhaps that of Abuela's Pedro.
It was meant to feel scary and threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.320
although as the camera drew closer,&nbsp;
it would be revealed to actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.200
the remaining cracks of the house. 
A potentially confusing alternate ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.400
for Camilo was considered at one time where&nbsp;
according to Jared Bush, after the house fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.760
and the magic died, Camilo "reverted to his&nbsp;
true self [...] and no one recognized him."
&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.480
That ending would have meant that the appearance&nbsp;
we saw Camilo use throughout the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.520
when he wasn't shapeshifting wasn't&nbsp;
actually his real form, it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.320
another character he was pretending to be.
I can see this ending for Camilo could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.760
been too distracting given the number&nbsp;
of questions it would have raised and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.920
as Bush explained, it was scrapped because&nbsp;
"the moment had to be about Mirabel".
&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:59.040
The casita also took on some creepy, ghost-like&nbsp;
characteristics in this unused concept art where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.320
the house revealed there was something wrong by&nbsp;
having all the paintings and pictures go blank.
&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.440
The idea here was that Casita was sick&nbsp;
in a way and was experiencing the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.320
memory loss or brain fog that people can get&nbsp;
sometimes when they're sick or suffering.
&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.720
Another way to represent the magic going&nbsp;
wrong was this scene where the yellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.520
butterflies would have all gone dark giving&nbsp;
the moment a very spooky and creepy vibe.
&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.720
And these unused pieces of concept art&nbsp;
show how, in another version of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.280
Casita could literally float above the&nbsp;
ground, both before it was destroyed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.400
later again when Mirabel restores it.
A deleted scene released by Disney on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.040
Encanto Blu-ray appears to follow up on&nbsp;
the confrontation I mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.240
between Mirabel and her mother Julieta.
The cut scene takes place after a devastated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.640
Mirabel has fled the Encanto, filled with&nbsp;
guilt and regret for how she believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.120
she's to blame for the state of the family,&nbsp;
and Alma decides to go in search of her.
&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.760
Abuela sets out on her own, promising that&nbsp;
when she returns and Julieta has healed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.880
they will repair the family stronger than ever.
After Abuela arrives in her former home town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:12.480
she discovers a mural dedicated to her beloved&nbsp;
Pedro with the words "To Love At All Is To Love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.760
Entirely" and a painting of him in the river with&nbsp;
his arms held up, which honours the sacrifice he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.480
made for the rest of the town when he confronted&nbsp;
the group of soldiers about to attack them.
&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.560
Abuela wells up with emotion at the&nbsp;
memory of Pedro and remains at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.520
mural overnight until the following day&nbsp;
a child tells her where Mirabel is. 
&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.920
And in the alternate ending, Abuela also confesses&nbsp;
how after Pedro's death she held on too tight to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.880
her family, afraid to lose them again.
After I lost him, I was so afraid.
&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.640
I couldn't bear the thought&nbsp;
of losing anyone else.
&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:57.600
And I realise now that my husband would be...
so disappointed, because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:03.040
all the people he would have wished&nbsp;
to understand his words, it was me. 
&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.520
And I am so sorry. 
A touch of this did make it into the final film:
&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.120
I was given a miracle.
A second chance.
&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:20.960
And I was so afraid to lose it that I&nbsp;
lost sight of who our miracle was for.
&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.840
And I am so sorry.
There's also a fascinating alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.280
reconciliation between Mirabel and Abuela&nbsp;
hinted at in this concept art where Alma's hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.400
transformed into a river that ran for miles and&nbsp;
Mirabel had to find and follow the path of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.240
river in order to reach her grandmother.
The idea here was to explore Abuela's PTSD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.320
following her husband's death, and I think&nbsp;
the fantastically long river would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.920
been a metaphor for the depth of Alma's sorrow&nbsp;
and how hard it was to reach her emotionally.
&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.440
The concept of Abuela's hair as a river also fits&nbsp;
nicely with Colombia's famous magical realism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.040
which also inspired the rest of movie, including&nbsp;
the yellow butterflies and magical rooms.
